                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
          %h1#section_table_of_contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statements"} Statements
              %li
                %a{:href => "#proofs"} Proofs
              %li
                %a{:href => "#corollaries"} Corollaries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#variation"} Variation
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#uniform_states_are_unique"} Uniform states are unique
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#statements Statements
          %p
            In its original form, the
            %em Eckmann–Hilton argument
            shows that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+monoids"} category of monoids
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Groups"} of groups
            is necessarily
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} commutative/abelian
          %p
            In other words this says that when a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            for the other, then the two structures actually coincide and the resulting monoid is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative
          %p
            From the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
          #2cat.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in C
              an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_x
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(Id_x)
              for the set of
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endo
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphisms
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+composition"} vertical composition
                  define the same
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
                  structure on
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi End
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Id
                            %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(Id_x)
              %li
                %p
                  this is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
          %p
            On the face of it, this is a special case of the general situation, although in fact every case is an example for appropriate
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            A more general version is this: If a set is equipped with two binary operations with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+elements"} identity elements
          %ol
            %li the other is also a homomorphism with respect to the first;
            %li the identities are the same;
            %li the operations are the same;
            %li the operation is commutative;
            %li the operation is associative.
          %p
            This can also be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internalised
            in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AguiarMahajan"} Aguiar and Mahajan
          %h2#proofs Proofs
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} String diagrams
            allow an almost trivial proof. Since there is only one object, and the only 1-morphism is the identity, the diagram of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ b
            (vertical composition) is simply two dots labelled
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            arranged vertically. This diagram can be morphed continuously to a horizontal arrangement, which is the diagram for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a * b
            (horizontal composition). This is then morphed to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            below
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \circ a
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-proof" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagram
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2cat"}
            is depicted in
            %a{:href => "#Cheng"} Cheng
            below. Here we prove the
            = succeed "-part" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The basic equation that we have (that one operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              is a homomorphism with respect to another operation
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∘
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo *
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \circ b) * (c \circ d) = (a * c) \circ (b * d) .
            %p
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi End
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(Id_x)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exchange+law"} exchange law
            %p We prove the list of results from above in order:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Simply read the basic equation backwards to see that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∘
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
                  is a homomorphism with respect to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              %li
                %p Then
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo *
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo *
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo *
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_\star = 1_\star * 1_\star = (1_\star \circ 1_\circ) * (1_\circ \circ 1_\star) = (1_\star * 1_\circ) \circ (1_\circ * 1_\star) = 1_\circ \circ 1_\circ = 1_\circ ,
                %p
                  so the identities are the same; we will now write this identity simply as
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %li
                %p Now
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo *
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo *
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo *
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a * b = (a \circ 1) * (1 \circ b) = (a * 1) \circ (1 * b) = a \circ b ,
                %p so the operations are the same; we will write them both with concatenation.
              %li
                %p Then
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b = (1 a) (b 1) = (1 b) (a 1) = b a ,
                %p so this operation is commutative.
              %li
                %p Finally,
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi c
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a b) c = (a b) (1 c) = (a 1) (b c) = a (b c) ,
                %p so the operation is associative.
          %p
            If you start with a monoid object in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mon
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo *
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a * b = (a \circ 1) * (1 \circ b) = (a * 1) \circ (1 * b) = a \circ b = (1 * a) \circ (b * 1) = (1 * b) \circ (a * 1) = b * a ,
          %p where the desired results involve the first, middle, and last expressions.
          %h2#corollaries Corollaries
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"} tuply monoidal
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+connected+n-category"} simply connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-tuply" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
          %h2#variation Variation
          %p
            There are variations on the Eckmann-Hilton argument that do not assume units. For example, if a set is equipped with two symmetric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a * b = b * a)
            and idempotent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a * a = a)
            binary operations that commute with each other, then the operations coincide.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo *
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a * b = (a * b) + (a * b) = (a * b) + (b * a) = (a + b) * (b + a) = (a + b) * (a + b) = a + b.
          %p
            For example, we might consider the algebraic theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattices
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 0.5
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a * b=c_{0.5}(a,b)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0.25
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0.5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0.5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0.5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{0.25}(a,b)=c_{0.5}(c_{0.5}(a,b),b)=(a*b)*b=a*(b*b)=a*b=c_{0.5}(a,b).
          %p
            This is relevant in computer science because probability is modelled by the free convex spaces monad, and non-determinism is modelled by the free semilattice monad. These monads are both
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+powerset+of+distributions+monad"} convex powerset of distributions monad
            is not commutative.
          %h3#uniform_states_are_unique Uniform states are unique
          %p
            A related guise of this result is the following: If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+monoidal+category"} distributive monoidal category
            whose unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is terminal, then any two morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                  %mo +
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p, q : I \to I + I
            which are symmetric, i.e. invariant under the swap
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo +
                    %mi I
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi I
                    %mo +
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I + I \cong I+I
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category-theoretic+approaches+to+probability+theory"} categorical probability
          %p
            Every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : I \to I + \ldots + I
            gives rise to an
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_p : C(X,Y)^n \to C(X,Y)
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_p(f_1,\ldots,f_n) = X \xrightarrow{p \otimes X} (I + \ldots + I) \otimes X \cong X + \ldots + X \xrightarrow{[f_1,\ldots,f_n]} Y
          %p
            The operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_q
            are idempotent (by terminality of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
          %p
            We recover the connection to algebraic theories if we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative
            and affine.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-space"} H-space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exchange+law"} exchange law
          %h2#references References
          %p Due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"} Peter Hilton
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structure maps in group theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/fundamenta-mathematicae/all/50/2/94854/structure-maps-in-group-theory"} doi:10.4064/fm-50-2-207-221
          %p reviewed (somewhat imperfectly) in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"} Peter Hilton
              = succeed "," do
                %em Group-like structures in general categories I multiplications and comultiplications
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01451367"} doi:10.1007/BF01451367
          %p Expositions:
          %ul#Cheng
            %li#Catsters
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Catsters"} The Catsters
              %p
                %em Eckmann-Hilton 1
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rjdo-RWQVIY"}> video
                )
              %p
                %em Eckmann-Hilton 2
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wnRqo7UHa-k"}> video
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"}> Eugenia Cheng
                , (
                %a{:href => "http://eugeniacheng.com/ehclock/"}> picture
                )
          %ul#Stacey
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew Stacey
              ,
              %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20150326221729/http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/documents/eckmannhilton.mpg"} animation
              (mpeg)
          %p
            Formulation in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UF-IAS-2012"}> Univalent Foundations Project
                , Thm. 2.1.6 in:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
                2013 (
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/book/"}> web
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2013/03/hott-online-323-g28e4374.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kristina+Sojakova"}> Kristina Sojakova
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Syllepsis in Homotopy Type Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Sojakova_Syllepsis_20210728.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Generalization to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            via
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operads"} (∞,1)-operads
          %ul
            %li#Batanin
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Batanin"}> Michael Batanin
                ,
                %em The Eckmann-Hilton argument and higher operads
                , Adv. Math. 217 (2008), no. 1, 334–385; (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0207281"}> arXiv:math.CT/0207281
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tomer+Schlank"}> Tomer Schlank
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lior+Yanovski"} Lior Yanovski
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The ∞-Categorical Eckmann-Hilton Argument
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06006"} arXiv:1808.06006
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/agt.2019.19.3119"} doi:10.2140/agt.2019.19.3119
          %p
            Formulation in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+categories"} braided monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li#AguiarMahajan
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Aguiar"}> Marcelo Aguiar
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Swapneel+Mahajan"} Swapneel Mahajan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Monoidal Functors, Species and Hopf Algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 29
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/view?ProductCode=CRMM/29"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4776-3
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~swapneel/aguiar.pdf"} pdf
